["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Upland birds live in small, exact places. Learn to see the brushy edge, the aspen draw and the berry slope, and you stop walking empty country.</p></aside><ul><li>This is a photo walk through four kinds of bird cover near our bases (Tip).</li><li>California quail: Okanagan valley bottoms, farms and towns <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Ruffed grouse: aspen, creek bottoms and mixed forest edges <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Dusky and spruce grouse: higher conifer forest, ridges and the subalpine <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Bird ID is in <a href=\"#/s/grouse\">Grouse</a> and <a href=\"#/s/upland-birds\">Quail, chukar, partridge and pheasant</a>. This session is the places.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-quail-vernon.jpg\" alt=\"California quail cock near Vernon. Quail perch on a post or stump to watch for danger.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>California quail cock near Vernon. Quail perch on a post or stump to watch for danger. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Alan D. Wilson, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:California_Quail_(Male).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-woods-rose-slope.jpg\" alt=\"Wild rose along a dry creek. Thorny thickets are quail hiding cover.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Wild rose along a dry creek. Thorny thickets are quail hiding cover. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Thayne Tuason, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rosa_woodsii_along_Douglas_Creek,_Douglas_County_Washington.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-blackberry.jpg\" alt=\"Blackberry thicket by a river. A covey can vanish into this.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Blackberry thicket by a river. A covey can vanish into this. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Thayne Tuason, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rubus_armeniacus_1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-chokecherry.jpg\" alt=\"Chokecherry in fruit. Brushy draws with berries hold quail and grouse.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Chokecherry in fruit. Brushy draws with berries hold quail and grouse. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Thayne Tuason, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prunus_virginiana_fruittwo-9-08-04.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>California quail live mainly in the Okanagan and Kettle valleys, mostly at 250 to 500 m and rarely above 750 m <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>They seldom go more than 15 to 30 m from thick cover <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Find the brush and you find the birds.</li></ul><ul><li>They favour farmland and suburbs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Most coveys sit on private land: ask first <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>There is no quail season in Region 2 (Lower Mainland) or Region 3 (Thompson) <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. Quail are a Region 8 (Okanagan) bird.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-aspen-grove-snow.jpg\" alt=\"Aspen grove in winter near Clearwater, BC. Ruffed grouse eat aspen buds all winter.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Aspen grove in winter near Clearwater, BC. Ruffed grouse eat aspen buds all winter. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Jason Hollinger, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aspen_Grove_in_Snow_(2276157824).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-dogwood-flowers.jpg\" alt=\"Red osier dogwood in flower. Its white berries feed grouse in fall.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Red osier dogwood in flower. Its white berries feed grouse in fall. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Robert Flogaus-Faust, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cornus_sericea_RF.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-snowberry.jpg\" alt=\"Snowberry. The berries hang on into winter for grouse.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Snowberry. The berries hang on into winter for grouse. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS Photo, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:File-Symphoricarpos_albus_-_native;-Blooms_June_-_July;_(9352ce2f-155d-4519-3e7c-5033029f6900).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/ruffed-grouse-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Ruffed grouse hen in the leaf litter. She blends right in.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Ruffed grouse hen in the leaf litter. She blends right in. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Mdf, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bonasa-umbellus-001edit1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Ruffed grouse are most common at 750 to 1,250 m in BC <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>They stay in a small area all year, often 2.4 to 16 ha <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Aspen: smooth white bark, round leaves that shake in the wind <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Buds are winter grouse food <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Walk old roads and trails through aspen and berry brush in October. Grouse come to the edges to pick grit and clover (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-subalpine-forest.jpg\" alt=\"Subalpine forest of spruce and lodgepole pine, Manning Park. Spruce grouse country.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Subalpine forest of spruce and lodgepole pine, Manning Park. Spruce grouse country. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Tim Gage at Flickr, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Picea_engelmannii_Pinus_contorta.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-douglas-fir-stand.jpg\" alt=\"Open Douglas fir forest. Dusky grouse feed on the needles in winter.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Open Douglas fir forest. Dusky grouse feed on the needles in winter. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Lavin from Bozeman, Montana, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pseudotsuga_menziesii_subsp._glauca_(Interior_Douglas-fir),_Wild_Horse_Island,_Montana_1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-huckleberry-bush.jpg\" alt=\"Huckleberry shrubs on a rocky ridge. Late summer food for dusky grouse.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Huckleberry shrubs on a rocky ridge. Late summer food for dusky grouse. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Katja Schulz from Washington, D. C., USA, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountain_Huckleberry_(14891686107).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-kinnikinnick.jpg\" alt=\"Kinnikinnick berries on dry, open ground under pine.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Kinnikinnick berries on dry, open ground under pine. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Lavin from Bozeman, Montana, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arctostaphylos_uva-ursi_(5192873642).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Dusky grouse move uphill in fall into dense conifers and eat needles in winter <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Many dusky grouse are seen at 1,500 to 2,250 m <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Spruce grouse are conifer specialists that eat pine and spruce needles <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Spruce grouse sit tight and trust their camouflage. Look up and look slowly (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-grouse-tracks-snow.jpg\" alt=\"Ruffed grouse tracks winding through soft snow.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Ruffed grouse tracks winding through soft snow. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Yellowstone National Park, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruffed_grouse_tracks_in_snow_(49522761717).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-grouse-track-line.jpg\" alt=\"A grouse track line: 3 forward toes, one print in front of the next.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A grouse track line: 3 forward toes, one print in front of the next. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grouse_Tracks_in_the_snow_(5547c3a5-6256-4c63-b589-8585e510ebaf).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-grouse-snow-roost.jpg\" alt=\"Snow roost hole with wing marks. A ruffed grouse burst out of here.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Snow roost hole with wing marks. A ruffed grouse burst out of here. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Cephas, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bonasa_umbellus_snow_hole.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-grouse-wing-prints.jpg\" alt=\"Wing prints in the snow where a grouse took off or landed.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Wing prints in the snow where a grouse took off or landed. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: John Good, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grouse_wing_prints_in_snow.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Grouse tracks show 3 long toes forward and a short one back, about 5 cm long <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>In deep, soft snow ruffed grouse dive in and roost under the snow for warmth <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>A hole with wing marks beside it means the bird flew out from under the snow <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Fresh tracks in new snow mean the bird is close. Follow slowly and be ready (Tip).</li></ul><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Grandpa's rule (wisdom): Hunt the edges, not the middle. Birds live where two kinds of cover meet.</p></aside>","<aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Walking the open field for quail. They are in the brush beside it.</li><li>Hunting grouse in dark timber in October when they are out on the berry and aspen edges.</li><li>Rushing a fresh track line in snow. Slow down; the bird is often within 50 m (Tip).</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Find one aspen stand with berry shrubs on its edge. Walk its border slowly at mid morning. Note every grouse you flush, the plant it came from and the height. Write it in the Journal.</p></aside>","<ul><li>BC Breeding Bird Atlas species accounts: <a href=\"https://www.birdatlas.bc.ca/accounts/speciesaccount.jsp?sp=CAQU&amp;lang=en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">California quail</a>, <a href=\"https://www.birdatlas.bc.ca/accounts/speciesaccount.jsp?sp=RUGR&amp;lang=en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ruffed grouse</a>, <a href=\"https://www.birdatlas.bc.ca/accounts/speciesaccount.jsp?sp=DUGR&amp;lang=en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">dusky grouse</a>, spruce grouse, checked 2026-10-03 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>California Department of Fish and Wildlife, California quail life history, checked 2026-10-03 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>: distance from cover.</li></ul><ul><li>Vermont Fish and Wildlife, ruffed grouse home range, checked 2026-10-03 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Audubon field guide, dusky grouse, checked 2026-10-03 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>: fall uphill move, winter needles.</li></ul><ul><li>Elbroch, Mammal Tracks and Sign, and Elbroch and Marks, Bird Tracks and Sign (Stackpole): grouse tracks and snow roosts (75% to 80%).</li></ul>","<ul><li>BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, 2 October 2026 edition, page 68: quail seasons only in Region 8 (my reading, 90%).</li></ul><ul><li>Photos: Wikimedia Commons, credits under each photo and on the Photo credits page. Checked 2026-10-04.</li></ul>"]